-1\\).  \nAll lines determined by two points of \\(S\\) are listed below together with their slopes.\n\n| Pair | Equation | Slope | Sunny? |\n|------|----------|-------|--------|\n| \\(A,B\\) | \\(x=1\\) | \\(\\infty\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(A,C\\) | \\(x=1\\) | \\(\\infty\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(A,D\\) | \\(y=1\\) | \\(0\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(A,E\\) | \\(y=x\\) | \\(1\\) | **sunny** |\n| \\(A,F\\) | \\(y=1\\) | \\(0\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(B,C\\) | \\(x=1\\) | \\(\\infty\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(B,D\\) | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\(-1\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(B,E\\) | \\(y=2\\) | \\(0\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(B,F\\) | \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) | \\(-\\tfrac12\\) | **sunny** |\n| \\(C,D\\) | \\(y=-2x+5\\) | \\(-2\\) | **sunny** |\n| \\(C,E\\) | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\(-1\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(C,F\\) | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\(-1\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(D,E\\) | \\(x=2\\) | \\(\\infty\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(D,F\\) | \\(y=1\\) | \\(0\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n| \\(E,F\\) | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\(-1\\) | non\u2011sunny |\n\nThus the only **sunny** lines determined by two points of \\(S\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:A\\!E\\;(y=x),\\qquad \n\\ell_{2}:B\\!F\\;(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52),\\qquad \n\\ell_{3}:C\\!D\\;(y=-2x+5).\n\\]\n\nEvery other line through two points of \\(S\\) is non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1.  Structure of a possible configuration\n\nWe need three distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) and exactly two of them must be sunny.  \nConsequently **exactly one** of the three lines is non\u2011sunny; the other two must be sunny (hence each must be one of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) or a sunny line that contains only a single point of \\(S\\)).\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(S\\); the only three\u2011point non\u2011sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nx=1\\;(A,B,C),\\qquad y=1\\;(A,D,F),\\qquad x+y=4\\;(C,E,F).\n\\]\n\nAll other non\u2011sunny lines contain at most two points of \\(S\\); the two\u2011point non\u2011sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nx=2\\;(D,E),\\qquad y=2\\;(B,E),\\qquad x+y=3\\;(B,D).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  The non\u2011sunny line contains three points  \n\nAssume the non\u2011sunny line is one of the three listed above and therefore covers three points of \\(S\\).  \nThe three points left uncovered are pairwise joined only by non\u2011sunny lines (see the table).  \nHence any sunny line can contain **at most one** of those three points.  \nTwo sunny lines can therefore cover at most two of them, leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nThus the non\u2011sunny line cannot contain three points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  The non\u2011sunny line contains exactly two points  \n\nWe examine each of the three possible two\u2011point non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n* **Case \\(x=2\\) (covers \\(D,E\\)).**  \n  Remaining points: \\(A,B,C,F\\).  \n  Among these, the only sunny pair is \\(B\\!F\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n  After using \\(B\\!F\\) as one sunny line, the points \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) are left; any line through them is vertical (non\u2011sunny).  \n  No second sunny line can cover both, and any other choice of sunny line still leaves at least one point uncovered.  \n\n* **Case \\(y=2\\) (covers \\(B,E\\)).**  \n  Remaining points: \\(A,C,D,F\\).  \n  The only sunny pair among them is \\(C\\!D\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n  After taking \\(C\\!D\\) as one sunny line, the points \\(A\\) and \\(F\\) remain; any line through \\(A\\) and \\(F\\) is horizontal (non\u2011sunny).  \n  Hence a second sunny line cannot cover both.\n\n* **Case \\(x+y=3\\) (covers \\(B,D\\)).**  \n  Remaining points: \\(A,C,E,F\\).  \n  The only sunny pair among them is \\(A\\!E\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  After using \\(A\\!E\\) as one sunny line, the points \\(C\\) and \\(F\\) remain; the line \\(C\\!F\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n  Again a second sunny line cannot cover both.\n\nIn each subcase the two sunny lines cannot cover the four remaining points, so a configuration with a two\u2011point non\u2011sunny line is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 4.  The non\u2011sunny line contains only one point  \n\nIf the non\u2011sunny line passes through a single point of \\(S\\), the two sunny lines can each contain at most two points of \\(S\\) (no sunny line contains three points, as seen from the table).  \nThus the two sunny lines together cover at most four of the remaining five points, leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nAll possible placements of the unique non\u2011sunny line lead to a contradiction.  \nTherefore **no** set of three distinct lines can cover the required six points with exactly two sunny lines when \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
