"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have rigorously proved that for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** configuration of three distinct lines that covers all required lattice points while having exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines. Hence a configuration with \\(k=2\\) sunny lines is impossible.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Points to be covered.** For \\(n=3\\) the condition \u201c\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)\u201d yields the six points  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Classification of lines through points of \\(S\\).**  \n   - A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and \\(-1\\).  \n   - By enumerating all \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) lines determined by two points of \\(S\\) we find that the only sunny lines containing two points of \\(S\\) are  \n     \\[\n     \\ell_{1}:y=x\\;(A\\!E),\\qquad \n     \\ell_{2}:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\;(B\\!F),\\qquad \n     \\ell_{3}:y=-2x+5\\;(C\\!D),\n     \\]\n     each of which contains exactly two points of \\(S\\).  \n   - Every other line through two points of \\(S\\) is non\u2011sunny (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n3. **Structure of a possible configuration.**  \n   We need three distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) with exactly two sunny lines; therefore **exactly one** of the three lines must be non\u2011sunny, the other two must be sunny (hence each is one of \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) or a sunny line that meets \\(S\\) in a single point).\n\n4. **Possible sizes of the non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   - A non\u2011sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(S\\). The only three\u2011point non\u2011sunny lines are  \n     \\[\n     x=1\\;(A,B,C),\\qquad y=1\\;(A,D,F),\\qquad x+y=4\\;(C,E,F).\n     \\]\n   - All other non\u2011sunny lines contain at most two points; the two\u2011point non\u2011sunny lines are  \n     \\[\n     x=2\\;(D,E),\\qquad y=2\\;(B,E),\\qquad x+y=3\\;(B,D).\n     \\]\n\n5. **Case