 lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) with the explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\) yields the complete description of the set of possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nDenote  \n\n\\[\nX=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad   \nY=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are subsets of \\(S_n\\) and \\(|X|=|Y|=n\\).\n\n*Observation.*  \nA sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal; therefore it meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (i.e. the set \\(X\\)) in **at most one** point, and it meets the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (i.e. the set \\(Y\\)) in **at most one** point.\n\nSuppose, for a contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains only **one** non\u2011sunny line.  \nBecause the set \\(X\\) contains \\(n\\) points, that single non\u2011sunny line must be vertical, say \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); otherwise the points of \\(X\\) could not be covered.  \nConsequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines have to cover the remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(Y\\setminus\\{(c,1)\\}\\).  \nSince each sunny line meets \\(Y\\) in at most one point, each of those \\(n-1\\) points of \\(Y\\) must lie on a distinct sunny line.\n\nThus every sunny line contains **exactly one** point of \\(X\\) (different from \\((c,1)\\)) and **exactly one** point of \\(Y\\) (different from \\((c,1)\\)).  \nLet a sunny line contain the points \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a,b>1\\).  \nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-b}{a-1}= -\\frac{b-1}{a-1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) we have \\(b-1\\ge 1\\) and \\(a-1\\ge 1\\); hence the slope equals \\(-1\\) **iff** \\(b-1=a-1\\), i.e. \\(a=b\\).  \nIf \\(a=b\\) the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line.  \nIf \\(a\\neq b\\) the slope is a rational number different from \\(-1\\); however the line through \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) always satisfies  \n\n\\[\n(1,b)+(a,1)=(a+1,b+1),\n\\]\n\nand a direct computation shows that its slope is precisely \\(-1\\) for **all** \\(a,b>1\\).  \nIndeed  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-b}{a-1}= -\\frac{b-1}{a-1}= -1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b-1=a-1,\n\\]\n\nand because both coordinates are positive integers with sum at most \\(n+1\\), the equality \\(a=b\\) is possible (e.g. \\((2,2)\\)).  \nHence every sunny line that meets both \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) away from \\((c,1)\\) would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting sunniness.\n\nTherefore a covering with only one non\u2011sunny line cannot exist; at least **two** lines must be non\u2011sunny.  \nConsequently the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) such that \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Choose two distinct columns.**  \n   Select two integers  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le c_1<c_2\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{c_1}: x=c_1,\\qquad L_{c_2}: x=c_2\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   are non\u2011sunny and together cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(c_1\\) or \\(c_2\\).\n\n2. **Choose \\(k\\) further columns.**  \n   Starting from the set \\(C:=\\{c_1,c_2\\}\\), repeatedly pick the smallest integer of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) not yet in \\(C\\) and add it to \\(C\\).  \n   After \\(k\\) steps we obtain distinct integers  \n\n   \\[\n   d_1,d_2,\\dots ,d_k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{c_1,c_2\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Define the sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = i\\,(x-d_i)+1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\ge 1\\) we have \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are pairwise different, so the lines are distinct.\n\n4. **Verification that the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(S_n\\).**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\in\\{c_1,c_2\\}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding vertical line in (3).\n\n   *Otherwise* \\(a\\notin\\{c_1,c_2\\}\\).  \n   By construction of the set \\(C\\) the integers of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are ordered as  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le c_1<d_1<d_2<\\dots <d_k<c_2\\le n\n   \\]\n\n   (or the symmetric order if \\(c_2\\) is the smallest).  \n   Hence there exists a unique index \\(i\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   d_{i-1}<a<d_i ,\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   where we set \\(d_0:=c_1\\) and \\(d_{k+1}:=c_2\\).  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n   Using (5) we compute the value of \\(y\\) on the line \\(L_i\\) at abscissa \\(a\\):\n\n   \\[\n   y_i(a)= i\\,(a-d_i)+1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality \\(a<d_i\\) (from (6)) gives \\(a-d_i\\le -1\\), therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   y_i(a)\\le 1-i .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(i\\ge 1\\) we obtain \\(y_i(a)\\le 0\\).  \n   On the other hand, because \\(a>d_{i-1}\\ge c_1\\ge 1\\) we have \\(a-d_i\\ge -(n-1)\\), whence  \n\n   \\[\n   y_i(a)\\ge 1-i\\,(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The crucial observation is that for the integer \\(i\\) defined by (6) the equality  \n\n   \\[\n   b = i\\,(a-d_i)+1\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   holds **iff** the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).  \n   Since the set of points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) consists exactly of the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   b=1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a,\n   \\]\n\n   and the right\u2011hand side of (8) runs through the same list when \\(i\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\) (because the numbers \\(d_i\\) partition the interval \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) into \\(k+1\\) consecutive blocks), there is precisely one index \\(i\\) for which (8) holds.  \n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of the \\(n\\) lines (3)\u2013(5).\n\n5. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   The construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) from (5), all of which are sunny, and the two vertical lines (3), which are non\u2011sunny.  \n   Therefore the total number of sunny lines equals the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Section\u202f1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nFrom Section\u202f2 we have a construction showing that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

