            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nDenote  \n\n\\[\nX=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad   \nY=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) are subsets of \\(S_n\\) and \\(|X|=|Y|=n\\).\n\n*Observation.*  \nA sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal; therefore it meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (i.e. the set \\(X\\)) in **at most one** point, and it meets the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (i.e. the set \\(Y\\)) in **at most one** point.\n\nSuppose, for a contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains only **one** non‑sunny line.  \nBecause the set \\(X\\) contains \\(n\\) points, that single non‑sunny line must be vertical, say \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); otherwise the points of \\(X\\) could not be covered.  \nConsequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines have to cover the remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(Y\\setminus\\{(c,1)\\}\\).  \nSince each sunny line meets \\(Y\\) in at most one point, each of those \\(n-1\\) points of \\(Y\\) must lie on a distinct sunny line.\n\nThus every sunny line contains **exactly one** point of \\(X\\) (different from \\((c,1)\\)) and **exactly one** point of \\(Y\\) (different from \\((c,1)\\)).  \nLet a sunny line contain the points \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a,b>1\\).  \nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-b}{a-1}= -\\frac{b-1}{a-1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) we have \\(b-1\\ge 1\\) and \\(a-1\\ge 1\\); hence the slope equals \\(-1\\) **iff** \\(b-1=a-1\\), i.e. \\(a=b\\).  \nIf \\(a=b\\) the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line.  \nIf \\(a\\neq b\\) the slope is a rational number different from \\(-1\\); however the line through \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) always satisfies  \n\n\\[\n(1,b)+(a,1)=(a+1,b+1),\n\\]\n\nand a direct computation shows that its slope is precisely \\(-1\\) for **all** \\(a,b>1\\).  \nIndeed  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-b}{a-1}= -\\frac{b-1}{a-1}= -1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b-1=a-1,\n\\]\n\nand because both coordinates are positive integers with sum at most \\(n+1\\), the equality \\(a=b\\) is possible (e.g. \\((2,2)\\)).  \nHence every sunny line that meets both \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) away from \\((c,1)\\) would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting sunniness.\n\nTherefore a covering with only one non‑sunny line cannot exist; at least **two** lines must be non‑sunny.  \nConsequently the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) such that \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. **Choose two distinct columns.**  \n   Select two integers  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le c_1<c_2\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{c_1}: x=c_1,\\qquad L_{c_2}: x=c_2\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   are