  \\]\n\n   The crucial observation is that for the integer \\(i\\) defined by (6) the equality  \n\n   \\[\n   b = i\\,(a-d_i)+1\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   holds **iff** the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).  \n   Since the set of points of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) consists exactly of the integers  \n\n   \\[\n   b=1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a,\n   \\]\n\n   and the right‑hand side of (8) runs through the same list when \\(i\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\) (because the numbers \\(d_i\\) partition the interval \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) into \\(k+1\\) consecutive blocks), there is precisely one index \\(i\\) for which (8) holds.  \n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of the \\(n\\) lines (3)–(5).\n\n5. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   The construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) from (5), all of which are sunny, and the two vertical lines (3), which are non‑sunny.  \n   Therefore the total number of sunny lines equals the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Section 1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nFrom Section 2 we have a construction showing that **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
