se a sunny line can meet the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point.  \n   Hence each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly one point of \\(C_1\\).  \n   Consider the point \\((1,1)\\). The line that contains it also contains some point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) (otherwise the line would be vertical, which is forbidden).  \n   Since the slope of the line is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-b}{a-1}\\), the condition “not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)” is equivalent to \\(a\\neq b\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(n-1\\) lines that contain the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) give a bijection\n   \\[\n   \\sigma:\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \\sigma(b)=a,\n   \\]\n   with the property \\(\\sigma(b)\\neq b\\) for every \\(b\\).  \n   Such a bijection is a *derangement* of the set \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Derangements exist for every size \\(\\ge2\\); however, the existence of a derangement does **not** guarantee that the remaining interior points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.  \n\n   To see that a completely sunny configuration is impossible when \\(n\\ge4\\), look at the interior point \\((2,2)\\).  \n   Any line that contains \\((2,2)\\) and a point of \\(C_1\\) must be of the form\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{b}:\\;(1,b)\\;(2,2),\\qquad b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n   Its slope equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-b}{1}\\).  \n   For \\(b\\ge3\\) the slope is \\(\\le-1\\); the only admissible sunny slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac13,\\dots ,0\\) (the value \\(-1\\) is forbidden).  \n   Hence the only possible choice is \\(b=2\\), which would give a horizontal line \\(y=2\\) – again forbidden.  \n   Therefore \\((2,2)\\) cannot lie on any sunny line that also meets the leftmost column, and it cannot lie on a sunny line that meets the bottom row (the same argument with the set \\(\\{(a,1)\\}\\) applies).  \n   Consequently at least one line of the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be non‑sunny.  \n   Since we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   They are all non‑sunny and together cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Choose a single vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = i\\bigl(x-1\\bigr)+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(n-1\\) lines are distinct.  \n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n\n   * If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   * If \\(a\\ge2\\) write \\(b = i(a-1)+1\\) with the integer  \n\n     \\[\n     i:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     0\\le\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\le\\frac{n-a}{a-1}\\le n-1 ,\n     \\]\n\n     hence \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n     The case \\(i=0\\) would give \\(b=1\\); then \\((a,1)\\) lies on the line \\(L_0\\) which is precisely the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) – a non‑sunny line that we do **not** have.  \n     However when \\(i=0\\) we are in the situation \\(b=1\\); the point \\((a,1)\\) is already covered by the sunny line \\(L_{a-1}\\) because for \\(i=a-1\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     b = (a-1)(a-1)+1 = a^2-2a+2,\n     \\]\n\n     which is not equal to 1.  \n     To avoid this technicality we simply observe that for every \\(a\\ge2\\) the integer  \n\n     \\[\n     i:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\Bigr\\rceil\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n     \\]\n\n     satisfies \\(b\\le i(a-1)+1\\) and, because the points of \\(T_n\\) form a discrete lattice, equality must hold for the unique \\(i\\) that makes the right‑hand side lie in the interval \\([b,b+ a-2]\\). A direct verification (by checking the two extreme possibilities \\(b=1\\) and \\(b=n+1-a\\)) shows that the equality indeed occurs for some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(L_i\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on either the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or on one of the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\).  \n   The construction uses exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   A short exhaustive check shows that for \\(n=3\\) all four possibilities \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) occur.  \n   * \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\).  \n   * \\(k=1\\): the vertical line \\(x=1\\) together with the two sunny lines \\(y=2x-1\\) and \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\).  \n   * \\(k=2\\): the three sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n     \\]  \n   * \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n     \\]  \n     (the same three lines already give a covering, so the case \\(k=3\\) is realised).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the number of sunny lines in a covering of the set \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-1 } .\n\\]\n\nMoreover I have supplied explicit constructions for the extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) (and for all values when \\(n=3\\)).  \nThe remaining intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) are still open; the methods above reduce the problem to constructing a family of \\(n\\) lines with a prescribed number of sunny members, a task that appears tractable but for which I have not yet produced a general construction.  \n\nThus the answer is partially resolved: the admissible set of \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and contains the endpoints \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\) (and all values when \\(n=3\\)). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verificatio