"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nand, in the exceptional case \\(n=3\\), the value \\(k=3\\) is also possible.  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   Assume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and that all of them are sunny.  \n   *The left\u2011most column* \\(C=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) contains \\(n\\) points.  \n   Because a sunny line is never vertical, each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) meets \\(C\\) in **at most one** point; consequently the \\(n\\) points of \\(C\\) must lie on \\(n\\) different lines. Hence every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly one point of \\(C\\).  \n\n   Consider the line \\(\\ell_{b}\\) that contains \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \n   Since \\(\\ell_{b}\\) is not horizontal, it also contains a point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\).  \n   The slope of \\(\\ell_{b}\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-b}{a-1}\\).  \n   The line is sunny, therefore \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-b}{a-1}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); the three forbidden values give respectively \\(b=1\\) (impossible because \\(b\\ge2\\)), \\(a=1\\) (impossible), and \\(a=b\\).  \n   Hence for each \\(b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we obtain a number \\(a=\\sigma(b)\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma(b)\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \\sigma(b)\\neq b .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The map \\(\\sigma:\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a bijection, because the \\(n-1\\) lines \\(\\ell_{b}\\) are distinct and each contains a different point of the bottom row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid a\\ge2\\}\\).  \n   Thus \\(\\sigma\\) is a **derangement** of the set \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n   Now look at the interior point \\((2,2)\\in T_n\\) (it exists for every \\(n\\ge4\\)).  \n   The line that contains \\((2,2)\\) must also contain a point of the left column, say \\((1,b)\\).  \n   Its slope is \\(2-b\\).  \n   If \\(b=2\\) the slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal, non\u2011sunny).  \n   If \\(b=3\\) the slope is \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), non\u2011sunny).  \n   For \\(b\\ge4\\) the slope is \\(\\le-2\\), which is allowed, but then the line would also contain the point \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge4\\).  \n   Because of (1) the only column point that can be paired with a bottom\u2011row point different from it is \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(\\sigma(b)\\neq b\\).  \n   In a derangement of \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the element \\(2\\) is sent to some \\(\\sigma(2)\\neq2\\); consequently the line through \\((1,2)\\) meets the bottom row at \\((\\sigma(2),1)\\) with \\(\\sigma(2)\\ge3\\).  \n   The slope of that line is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-2}{\\sigma(2)-1}= -\\frac{1}{\\sigma(2)-1}\\), whose absolute value is at most \\(\\frac12\\).  \n   Hence this line cannot pass through \\((2,2)\\) (its slope would have to be \\(0\\)).  \n\n   The only remaining possibility for covering \\((2,2)\\) is a line that meets the **bottom row** at a point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\).  \n   By the same reasoning as above, the line through \\((a,1)\\) also meets the left column at \\((1,\\tau(a))\\) where \\(\\tau\\) is the inverse of \\(\\sigma\\); the slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-\\tau(a)}{a-1}\\).  \n   For \\(a\\ge3\\) this slope is \\(\\le -\\frac12\\) and never equals \\(0\\).  \n   Consequently \\((2,2)\\) cannot be covered by any sunny line when \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n\n   Hence a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines **must contain at least one non\u2011sunny line** for every \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\ge4).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=3\\) the set \\(T_3\\) consists of six points; the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \n   \\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad \n   \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are all sunny and together cover \\(T_3\\). Hence \\(k=3\\) is possible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n3. **Construction for the lower bound \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n   \\]\n\n   are all non\u2011sunny and clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\). Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n4. **Construction for the upper bound \\(k=n-1\\) (all but one line sunny).**  \n   Fix the non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\).  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = (i-1)(x-1)+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\); it is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n-1\\) lines are distinct.  \n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\le\\frac{n-a}{a-1}\\le n-1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   hence \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n   By definition of the ceiling,  \n\n   \\[\n   i-1<\\frac{b-1}{a-1}\\le i .\n   \\]\n\n   Multiplying by \\(a-1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (i-1)(a-1)<b-1\\le i(a-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Since all numbers are integers, the right\u2011hand inequality forces equality:\n\n   \\[\n   b-1 = i(a-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{i+1}\\) (recall that the index in (3) is \\(i+1\\)).  \n   Thus every point with \\(a\\ge2\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\).  \n   The points with \\(a=1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   Hence the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,x=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is non\u2011sunny, and it covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).  \n   Therefore \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Construction for an arbitrary intermediate value \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be given.  \n   *Step\u202f1.* Use the non\u2011sunny vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   *Step\u202f2.* For the first \\(k\\) indices \\(i=2,\\dots ,k+1\\) keep the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) defined in (3).  \n   *Step\u202f3.* For the remaining indices \\(i=k+2,\\dots ,n\\) replace \\(L_i\\) by the horizontal line \\(y=i-1\\) (which is non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   The resulting family contains  \n\n   * one vertical line (non\u2011sunny),  \n   * \\(k\\) lines of the form \\(L_i\\) (sunny),  \n   * \\(n-1-k\\) horizontal lines (non\u2011sunny).\n\n   Hence exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   \u2013 Points with \\(a=1\\) are on the vertical line.  \n   \u2013 Points with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\le k\\) satisfy \\(b-1\\le k\\le i-1\\) for some \\(i\\le k+1\\); the same ceiling argument as in part\u202f4 shows that they belong to one of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\le k+1\\).  \n   \u2013 Points with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b>k\\) have \\(b\\ge k+1\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n-k\\). Hence \\(a\\le n-k\\) and consequently \\(b\\) equals the constant \\(i-1\\) for the horizontal line \\(y=i-1\\) with \\(i=b+1\\) (which lies in the range \\(k+2\\le i\\le n\\)). Thus such points lie on the appropriate horizontal line.  \n\n   All points of \\(T_n\\) are therefore covered, and the construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the constructions of Sections\u202f3,\u202f4 and\u202f5 give the values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge4\\) the upper bound (2) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-1\\); the constructions of Sections\u202f3,\u202f4 and\u202f5 show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is realizable.\n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated at the beginning of the summary. \u220e"

