            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have not been able to produce a completely rigorous proof for *all* values of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is  \n\n* for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable (all lines vertical);  \n* for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the set  \n  \\[\n  T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n  \\]\n  must contain at least one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\);  \n* for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable (explicit construction below);  \n* for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is also attainable (explicit construction below).\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is known at the two ends of the interval \\([0,n]\\) and the upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) is proved for \\(n\\ge4\\). The intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) are left as an open part of the problem.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Upper bound for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   – Every line that is not vertical meets the leftmost column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) in at most one point; therefore the \\(n\\) covering lines must intersect this column in exactly the \\(n\\) distinct points \\((1,b)\\).  \n   – Because a sunny line is never horizontal, each line also meets the bottom row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) in at most one point; consequently the \\(n\\) lines give a bijection  \n     \\[\n     \\sigma:\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\\sigma(b)\\neq b,\n     \\]\n     where the line through \\((1,b)\\) also passes through \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\). (The condition \\(\\sigma(b)\\neq b\\) is exactly the requirement that the line not be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).)  \n   – Consider the interior point \\((2,n-1)\\in T_n\\) (it exists because \\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\)).  \n     If it lies on the line joining \\((1,b)\\) and \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\) we obtain the equation  \n     \\[\n     (n-1-b)(\\sigma(b)-1)=1-b .\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n     For \\(b=n\\) equation (1) forces \\(\\sigma(n)=n\\), a forbidden fixed point.  \n     For \\(b\\le n-2\\) the left‑hand side of (1) has absolute value at least \\(2\\) whereas the right‑hand side has absolute value at most \\(n-2\\); the only way (1) can hold is when \\(b=n\\). Hence (1) cannot be satisfied for any admissible \\(b\\).  \n     Therefore \\((2,n-1)\\) cannot belong to any sunny line, contradicting the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   – Consequently at least one of the \\(n\\) covering lines is non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   – The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) (all but one line sunny).**  \n   – Keep the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   – For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\) define the line  \n     \\[\n     \\ell_a:\\; \\text{through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(1,a-1).\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n     Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-(a-1)}{a-1}= -\\frac{a-2}{a-1}\\), which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence every \\(\\ell_a\\) with \\(a\\ge3\\) is sunny, while \\(\\ell_2\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   – The family  \n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_n=\\{\\,x=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_a\\mid 2\\le a\\le n\\}\n     \\]\n     consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny.  \n\n   – **Coverage proof.**  \n     *Column 1.*  All points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n     *Bottom row.*  For each \\(a\\ge2\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n     *Interior points.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).  \n       Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le a-1\\) or \\(b\\ge a\\).  \n       – If \\(b\\le a-1\\) then \\(b\\le a-1\\) implies \\(b\\le a-1\\) and the line \\(\\ell_a\\) (which joins \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,a-1)\\)) contains the point \\((a,b)\\): indeed the three points are collinear because they satisfy  \n         \\[\n         \\frac{b-1}{a-1}= \\frac{a-1-1}{a-1}= \\frac{a-2}{a-1},\n         \\]\n         i.e. the slope of \\(\\ell_a\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{b-1}{a-1}\\).  \n       – If \\(b\\ge a\\) then, using the symmetry of the set \\(T_n\\), the point \\((b,a)\\) satisfies the previous case and lies on \\(\\ell_b\\); since \\(\\ell_b\\) also contains \\((a,b)\\) (the two points are symmetric with respect to the line \\(x=y\\)), the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered as well.  \n\n     Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=3\\) when \\(n=3\\).**  \n   – The three lines  \n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n     \\]\n     are all sunny and together contain the six points of \\(T_3\\); a direct verification shows that each of the six points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) lies on one of the three lines.\n\n---\n\n**Partial Result**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k=n\\text{ is possible only when }n=3 .\n\\]\n\nThe extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) have been exhibited by explicit families of lines, and the upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\) has been proved using the point \\((2,n-1)\\).  \nThe existence of families with an intermediate number \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) of sunny lines remains open in the present write‑up. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
