ow at a distinct point\u202f(a,1) with\u202fa\u202f\u2265\u202f2, and that no line meets (1,1). The solution never shows that (1,1) is covered by a non\u2011sunny line nor that the mapping is bijective; thus the permutation claim is a **justification gap**.\n* The assertion that the interior point\u202f(2,\u202fn\u20111) cannot belong to any sunny line is **unsupported**. No algebraic or geometric argument is provided to exclude the possibility that some sunny line through (1,b) and (a,1) might also pass through (2,\u202fn\u20111). Hence this is another **justification gap**.\n\nBecause the upper\u2011bound argument rests on these unproved statements, the conclusion\u202fk\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u20111 is not rigorously established.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Construction for\u202fk\u202f=\u202f0 (second bullet)\n\n> **Quoted text:** \u201cThe\u202fn\u202fvertical lines\u202fx\u202f=\u202f1,\u202fx\u202f=\u202f2,\u202f\u2026,\u202fx\u202f=\u202fn are all non\u2011sunny and clearly cover every point of\u202fT\u2099.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n* Each vertical line\u202fx\u202f=\u202fa (1\u202f\u2264\u202fa\u202f\u2264\u202fn) contains all points with first coordinate\u202fa. Since for any point\u202f(a,b)\u2208T\u2099 we have\u202f1\u202f\u2264\u202fa\u202f\u2264\u202fn, the point lies on the line\u202fx\u202f=\u202fa.  \n* The lines are distinct and parallel to the\u202fy\u2011axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n* Therefore this construction is **correct**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Construction for\u202fk\u202f=\u202fn\u20111 (third bullet)\n\n> **Quoted text (definition of the family):**  \n> \u201cNon\u2011sunny line. Take the vertical line\u202f\u2113\u2080:\u202fx\u202f=\u202f1.  \n> Sunny lines. For each integer\u202fa\u202fwith\u202f2\u202f\u2264\u202fa\u202f\u2264\u202fn define  \n> \\[\n> \\ell_a:\\text{ the line through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(1,a-1).\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]  \n> For\u202fa\u202f\u2265\u202f3 the slope of\u202f\u2113\u2090 equals \\(-\\dfrac{a-2}{a-1}\\); it is neither\u202f0,\u202f\u221e\u202fnor\u202f\u20111, hence\u202f\u2113\u2090 is sunny.  \n> The line\u202f\u2113\u2082 is horizontal and therefore non\u2011sunny; we simply omit it.  \n> Consequently the family  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L_n=\\{\\ell_0\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_a\\mid 3\\le a\\le n\\}\n> \\]  \n> consists of exactly\u202fn\u202fdistinct lines, one of which (\u2113\u2080) is non\u2011sunny and the remaining\u202fn\u20111\u202fare sunny.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**\n* **Counting error (Critical Error):** The set\u202f\\(\\{\\ell_0\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_a\\mid 3\\le a\\le n\\}\\) contains\u202f1\u202f+\u202f(n\u20112)\u202f=\u202fn\u20111\u202flines, not\u202fn. The claim \u201cexactly\u202fn\u202fdistinct lines\u201d is false.\n* **Missing coverage (Critical Error):** By omitting\u202f\u2113\u2082, the point\u202f(2,\u202f1) (which belongs to\u202fT\u2099) is not on any line of\u202f\\(\\mathcal L_n\\). No other line in the family passes through\u202f(2,\u202f1). Hence the family does **not** cover all points of\u202fT\u2099.\n* **Conclusion:** The construction for\u202fk\u202f=\u202fn\u20111 is invalid; it fails both the cardinality requirement and the covering requirement. This is a **critical error** that invalidates the claimed existence of a configuration with\u202fk\u202f=\u202fn\u20111.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Coverage argument for the family\u202f\\(\\mathcal L_n\\) (sub\u2011bullet under the construction)\n\n> **Quoted text:**  \n> \u201cCoverage. \u2013 Column\u202f1. All points\u202f(1,b)\u202f(1\u202f\u2264\u202fb\u202f\u2264\u202fn) lie on\u202f\u2113\u2080. \u2013 Bottom row. For each\u202fa\u202f\u2265\u202f3 the point\u202f(a,1) lies on\u202f\u2113\u2090. \u2013 Interior points. Let\u202f(a,b)\u2208T\u2099 with\u202fa\u22652,\u202fb\u22652. Because\u202fa+b\u2264n+1 we have either\u202fb\u2264a\u20111 or\u202fa\u2264b\u20111.  \n> *If\u202fb\u2264a\u20111*, then\u202f(a,b)\u202flies on\u202f\u2113\u2090: indeed the three points\u202f(a,1),\u202f(1,a\u20111),\u202f(a,b) are collinear because  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{b-1}{a-1}= \\frac{a-1-1}{a-1}= \\frac{a-2}{a-1},\n> \\]  \n> which is exactly the (negative) slope of\u202f\u2113\u2090.  \n> *If\u202fa\u2264b\u20111*, then by symmetry the point\u202f(b,a) satisfies the previous case and lies on\u202f\u2113_b; the line\u202f\u2113_b also contains\u202f(a,b) because the two points are symmetric with respect to the line\u202fx\u202f=\u202fy.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**\n* The claim that **every** interior point with\u202fb\u202f\u2264\u202fa\u20111 lies on\u202f\u2113\u2090 is **false**. The equality  \n  \\[\n  \\frac{b-1}{a-1}= \\frac{a-2}{a-1}\n  \\]  \n  holds **only** when\u202fb\u20111\u202f=\u202fa\u20112, i.e.\u202fb\u202f=\u202fa\u20111. For any smaller\u202fb (e.g.\u202fb\u202f=\u202fa\u20112) the equality fails, so the three points are not collinear. Consequently many interior points are not covered by the proposed lines.\n* The symmetry argument for the case\u202fa\u202f\u2264\u202fb\u20111 suffers from the same flaw; it only guarantees coverage of points with\u202fa\u202f=\u202fb\u20111, not all points with\u202fa\u202f\u2264\u202fb\u20111.\n* Because the interior\u2011point coverage is incorrect, even if the family had the correct number of lines, it would **not** cover all points of\u202fT\u2099. This is a **critical error**.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Exceptional case\u202fn\u202f=\u202f3 (fourth bullet)\n\n> **Quoted text:** \u201cThe three lines  \n> \\[\n> y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n> \\]  \n> are all sunny and together contain the six points of\u202fT\u2083; therefore\u202fk\u202f=\u202f3\u202f=\u202fn is attainable when\u202fn\u202f=\u202f3.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**\n* The set\u202f\\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n  \\((1,1), (1,2), (1,3), (2,1), (2,2), (3,1)\\).\n* **Line\u202fy\u202f=\u202fx** passes through\u202f(1,1) and\u202f(2,2) (and (3,3) which is not in\u202fT\u2083).  \n* **Line\u202fy\u202f=\u202f\u2011\u00bdx\u202f+\u202f3/2** yields integer points only at\u202fx\u202f=\u202f1 (y\u202f=\u202f1). Thus it adds no new point of\u202fT\u2083 beyond (1,1).  \n* **Line\u202fy\u202f=\u202f\u20112x\u202f+\u202f5** passes through\u202f(1,3) and\u202f(2,1).  \n* The points\u202f(1,2) and\u202f(3,1) are **not** on any of the three lines. Hence the three lines do **not** cover all points of\u202fT\u2083.\n* Therefore the claim that\u202fk\u202f=\u202f3 is attainable for\u202fn\u202f=\u202f3 is **incorrect** \u2013 a **critical error**.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Impossibility of\u202fk\u202f=\u202fn\u202f(for\u202fn\u202f\u2265\u202f4) (first part of the \u201cImpossibility of any other\u202fk\u201d bullet)\n\n> **Quoted text:** \u201cIf\u202fk\u202f=\u202fn\u202fand\u202fn\u202f\u2265\u202f4: an all\u2011sunny family would consist only of lines that meet the leftmost column and the bottom row each in a single point, giving a fixed\u2011point\u2011free permutation \u03c3 of\u202f{2,\u2026,n}. As shown in the upper\u2011bound argument, the interior point\u202f(2,n\u20111) cannot belong to any such line, contradicting the covering requirement. Hence\u202fk\u202f=\u202fn is impossible for\u202fn\u202f\u2265\u202f4.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**\n* The impossibility proof relies on the **upper\u2011bound argument** that the interior point\u202f(2,\u202fn\u20111) cannot lie on any sunny line. As noted in step\u202f1, this claim lacks a rigorous justification. Consequently the impossibility proof inherits a **justification gap**.\n* Moreover, the statement that the mapping \u03c3 is a fixed\u2011point\u2011free permutation is not proved (see step\u202f1). Hence the argument is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Impossibility of\u202f1\u202f\u2264\u202fk\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u20112\u202f(for\u202fn\u202f\u2265\u202f4) (second part of the \u201cImpossibility of any other\u202fk\u201d bullet)\n\n> **Quoted text:** \u201cIf\u202f1\u202f\u2264\u202fk\u202f\u2264\u202fn\u20112\u202fand\u202fn\u202f\u2265\u202f4: suppose a family with exactly\u202fk\u