s. Let (a,b)∈Tₙ with a≥2, b≥2. Because a+b≤n+1 we have either b≤a‑1 or a≤b‑1.  \n> *If b≤a‑1*, then (a,b) lies on ℓₐ: indeed the three points (a,1), (1,a‑1), (a,b) are collinear because  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{b-1}{a-1}= \\frac{a-1-1}{a-1}= \\frac{a-2}{a-1},\n> \\]  \n> which is exactly the (negative) slope of ℓₐ.  \n> *If a≤b‑1*, then by symmetry the point (b,a) satisfies the previous case and lies on ℓ_b; the line ℓ_b also contains (a,b) because the two points are symmetric with respect to the line x = y.”\n\n**Verification:**\n* The claim that **every** interior point with b ≤ a‑1 lies on ℓₐ is **false**. The equality  \n  \\[\n  \\frac{b-1}{a-1}= \\frac{a-2}{a-1}\n  \\]  \n  holds **only** when b‑1 = a‑2, i.e. b = a‑1. For any smaller b (e.g. b = a‑2) the equality fails, so the three points are not collinear. Consequently many interior points are not covered by the proposed lines.\n* The symmetry argument for the case a ≤ b‑1 suffers from the same flaw; it only guarantees coverage of points with a = b‑1, not all points with a ≤ b‑1.\n* Because the interior‑point coverage is incorrect, even if the family had the correct number of lines, it would **not** cover all points of Tₙ. This is a **critical error**.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Exceptional case n = 3 (fourth bullet)\n\n> **Quoted text:** “The three lines  \n> \\[\n> y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n> \\]  \n> are all sunny and together contain the six points of T₃; therefore k = 3 = n is attainable when n = 3.”\n\n**Verification:**\n* The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n  \\((1,1), (1,2), (1,3), (2,1), (2,2), (3,1)\\).\n* **Line y = x** passes through (1,1) and (2,2) (and (3,3) which is not in T₃).  \n* **Line y = ‑½x + 3/2** yields integer points only at x = 1 (y = 1). Thus it adds no new point of T₃ beyond (1,1).  \n* **Line y = ‑2x + 5** passes through (1,3) and (2,1).  \n* The points (1,2) and (3,1) are **not** on any of the three lines. Hence the three lines do **not** cover all points 