            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,\\,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,\\,1,\\,3\\}, & n=3 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   The points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the leftmost column are  \n   \\(C=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\);  \n   those on the bottom row are  \n   \\(R=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\).  \n   \\(|C|=|R|=n\\) and \\(|C\\cup R|=2n-1\\).  \n   The interior points are \\(I=T_n\\setminus(C\\cup R)\\);  \n   \\(|I|=\\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\).\n\n2. **Basic observations about sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line is not vertical, not horizontal and not of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Consequently a sunny line meets \\(C\\) in at most one point and meets \\(R\\) in at most one point.\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is always possible.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) (all but one line sunny) – valid for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**  \n\n   *Non‑sunny line.*  Take the vertical line \\(\\ell_0:x=1\\); it contains all points of \\(C\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(3\\le a\\le n\\) define  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_a:\\text{ the line through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(1,a-1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_a\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{a-2}{a-1}\\); it is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny.  \n   The family  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_n=\\{\\ell_0\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_a\\mid 3\\le a\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, one of which (\\(\\ell_0\\)) is non‑sunny and the remaining \\(n-1\\) are sunny.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   – Every point of \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\ell_0\\).  \n   – For each \\(a\\ge3\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n   – Let \\((a,b)\\in I\\) (so \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n     If \\(b\\le a-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_a\\) because the three points\n     \\((a,1),(1,a-1),(a,b)\\) are collinear:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{b-1}{a-1}= \\frac{a-1-1}{a-1}= \\frac{a-2}{a-1},\n     \\]\n     which is exactly the (negative) slope of \\(\\ell_a\\).  \n     If \\(a\\le b-1\\) we apply the same argument with the symmetric line\n     \\(\\ell_b\\); the line \\(\\ell_b\\) also contains \\((a,b)\\) because the two\n     points are symmetric with respect to the line \\(x=y\\).  \n     Hence every interior point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the sunny lines\n     \\(\\ell_a\\;(a\\ge3)\\).  \n   Thus \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly\n   \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Maximum possible number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   *Lemma 5.1.*  If all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then at most one interior point can be covered by a sunny line that does **not** pass through a point of \\(C\\) or \\(R\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Let a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contain a point of \\(C\\) (say \\((1,b)\\)) and a point\n   of \\(R\\) (say \\((a,1)\\)).  Its equation can be written as  \n   \\[\n   \\frac{x-1}{a-1}+\\frac{y-1}{b-1}=1 .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   For an interior lattice point \\((u,v)\\) with \\(u,v\\ge2\\) we have\n   \\(0<u-1<a-1\\) and \\(0<v-1<b-1\\); consequently the left–hand side of (2) is\n   strictly smaller than \\(1\\).  Hence no interior point satisfies (2).  ∎\n\n   *Lemma 5.2.*  In any family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\) at least\n   one line must contain a point of \\(C\\) and a point of \\(R\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The set \\(C\\cup R\\) has \\(2n-1\\) points.  A sunny line can contain at most\n   one point of \\(C\\) and at most one point of \\(R\\); therefore a single\n   sunny line can cover at most two points of \\(C\\cup R\\).  With \\(n\\) lines we can\n   cover at most \\(2n\\) points of \\(C\\cup R\\).  Since \\(2n-1\\le2n\\), at least\n   one line must indeed meet both \\(C\\) and \\(R\\). ∎\n\n   *Theorem 5.3.*  For \\(n\\ge4\\) it is impossible to have all \\(n\\) lines sunny.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Assume, to the contrary, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny and cover \\(T_n\\).\n   By Lemma 5.2 there is a sunny line \\(\\ell_0\\) that meets \\(C\\) and \\(R\\); write\n   \\(\\ell_0\\) as in (2) with some \\(a,b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(a\\neq b\\)\n   (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)).  \n   Removing \\(\\ell_0\\), the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines each meet \\(C\\cup R\\)\n   in at most two points, so together they can cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points\n   of \\(C\\cup R\\).  Together with \\(\\ell_0\\) we obtain a total capacity of\n   \\(2n\\) points for \\(C\\cup R\\); the only point possibly left uncovered is\n   \\((1,1)\\).  Consequently every interior point of \\(I\\) must lie on one of the\n   \\(n-1\\) lines that do **not** contain a point of \\(C\\) and a point of \\(R\\).\n\n   By Lemma 5.1 each of those \\(n-1\\) lines can contain at most one interior\n   point (otherwise it would have to pass through a column point and a row\n   point, contradicting Lemma 5.1).  Hence the total number of interior points\n   that can be covered is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\n   However \\(|I|=\\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\ge3\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\).  Since\n   \\(n-1<|I|\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\), the interior points cannot all be covered – a\n   contradiction.  Therefore an all‑sunny family does not exist for \\(n\\ge4\\). ∎\n\n   The theorem shows that the maximal possible number of sunny lines is\n   \\(n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility for intermediate values of \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, where\n   \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Remove all non‑sunny lines; at most \\(n-k\\ge2\\) lines are\n   removed.  The remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines can meet \\(C\\) in at most \\(k\\) points\n   and meet \\(R\\) in at most \\(k\\) points, so at most \\(2k\\) points of\n   \\(C\\cup R\\) are covered.  Since \\(|C\\cup R|=2n-1\\) and \\(2k\\le2n-4\\) for\n   \\(k\\le n-2\\), at least three points of \\(C\\cup R\\) remain uncovered.\n   Those uncovered points cannot be supplied by the removed non‑sunny lines,\n   because each removed line is already counted among the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines and we have removed **all** of them.  Hence the whole set \\(T_n\\) cannot be\n   covered – a contradiction.  Therefore no configuration with\n   \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) exists for \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n7. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1),(2,2).\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The three vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,x=3\\) work.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Take the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); they cover all\n   points except \\((2,2)\\).  The sunny line \\(y=x\\) passes through \\((2,2)\\) and\n   does not coincide with the previous two lines.  Thus exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Use the three sunny lines\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5 .\n   \\]\n   One checks directly that each of the six