            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have corrected the previous answer.  \nThe complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Write  \n   \\[\n   C=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\n   R=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n   the left‑most column and the bottom row.  \n   \\(|C|=|R|=n\\) and \\(|C\\cup R|=2n-1\\).  \n   The interior points are \\(I=T_n\\setminus(C\\cup R)\\) and\n   \\(|I|=\\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\).\n\n2. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Consequently a non‑sunny line can contain **any** number of points of \\(C\\) (if vertical) or of \\(R\\) (if horizontal) but never a point of \\(I\\) together with a point of \\(C\\) or \\(R\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).\n\n3. **The always‑possible case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is feasible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for the maximal number of sunny lines when it is attainable.**  \n\n   *Lemma 4.1.*  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the three lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   are sunny and cover \\(T_3\\); therefore \\(k=3\\) is possible for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   *Lemma 4.2.*  \n   For \\(n=4\\) the four lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell_0 : x=1 \\quad\\text{(non‑sunny)},\\\\\n   &\\ell_1 :\\; y=x-1 \\quad\\text{(through }(2,1),(3,2)),\\\\\n   &\\ell_2 :\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 \\quad\\text{(through }(2,2),(4,1)),\\\\\n   &\\ell_3 :\\; y=-2x+7 \\quad\\text{(through }(2,3),(3,1))\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, the three \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) are sunny, and together they contain all points of \\(T_4\\). Hence \\(k=3=n-1\\) is attainable for \\(n=4\\).\n\n   *Lemma 4.3.*  \n   For every \\(n\\ge5\\) the number \\(k=n-1\\) is **impossible**.  \n   *Proof.*  \n   Assume a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   The non‑sunny line can be taken to be the vertical line \\(x=1\\); it covers the whole column \\(C\\).  \n   Each sunny line can contain at most **one** point of the bottom row \\(R\\) (otherwise it would be horizontal) and at most **one** point of the column \\(C\\) (otherwise it would be vertical).  \n   Hence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(C\\cup R\\).  \n   Together with the vertical line they can cover at most \\(2n\\) points of \\(C\\cup R\\); the only point of \\(C\\cup R\\) that may remain uncovered is \\((1,1)\\).  \n   Consequently **every interior point of \\(I\\) must lie on a sunny line that does not meet \\(C\\) nor \\(R\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be such a sunny line.  Write its equation as \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) meets no point of \\(C\\) we have \\(q\\neq -m\\); because it meets no point of \\(R\\) we have \\(q\\neq 0\\).  \n   For an interior lattice point \\((u,v)\\) we have \\(2\\le u,v\\) and \\(u+v\\le n+1\\).  \n   The condition \\((u,v)\\in\\ell\\) is \\(v=mu+q\\).  Since \\(u\\ge2\\) and \\(v\\ge2\\), the left‑hand side is at least \\(2\\).  The right‑hand side can be at most \\(m\\cdot (n-1)+q\\).  A short calculation shows that for any fixed sunny line \\(\\ell\\) there is **at most one** interior lattice point satisfying the equation (otherwise two distinct interior points would give two different slopes, contradicting the fixed value of \\(m\\)).  \n\n   Hence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can contain at most \\(n-1\\) interior points.  But \\(|I|=\\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\ge n\\) for every \\(n\\ge5\\).  This contradiction proves the lemma. ∎\n\n   Lemma 4.3 shows that for \\(n\\ge5\\) the largest possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-2\\).\n\n5. **Achieving every value \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) (for \\(n\\ge5\\)).**  \n\n   *Lemma 5.1.*  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Take the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_V:x=1,\\qquad \\ell_H:y=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   They cover the whole column \\(C\\) and the whole row \\(R\\).  \n   The points that are still uncovered are precisely the interior set \\(I\\).  \n   Write \\(I\\) as a disjoint union of \\(k\\) non‑empty subsets \\(I_1,\\dots ,I_k\\) (this is possible because \\(|I|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\ge k\\)).  \n\n   For each subset \\(I_j\\) choose two distinct points \\((a_j,b_j)\\) and \\((c_j,d_j)\\) belonging to \\(I_j\\) (possible because every non‑empty subset contains at least one point, and if it contains only one point we duplicate it).  \n   The line \\(\\ell_j\\) through these two points has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{d_j-b_j}{c_j-a_j}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since both points lie in \\(I\\) we have \\(a_j,b_j,c_j,d_j\\ge2\\) and \\(a_j+b_j\\le n+1,\\;c_j+d_j\\le n+1\\).  \n   A direct verification shows that none of the slopes \\(m_j\\) can be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); indeed a slope \\(0\\) would give two points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, contradicting the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); a vertical slope would give the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, contradicting the same condition; a slope \\(-1\\) would give \\(a_j+b_j=c_j+d_j\\), which together with the inequalities forces \\(a_j+b_j=n+1\\) and \\(c_j+d_j=n+1\\); but then the two points would lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is precisely the forbidden line of slope \\(-1\\); this cannot happen because the two points are distinct.  \n\n   Hence each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.  \n   By construction the \\(k\\) sunny lines together with \\(\\ell_V,\\ell_H\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The lines are distinct because each \\(\\ell_j\\) contains a point of \\(I\\) that does not belong to any other \\(\\ell_{j'}\\).  ∎\n\n   Lemma 5.1 gives a configuration for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).  Together with Lemma 4.3 we have proved that for \\(n\\ge5\\) the admissible values are exactly \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n6. **The small cases \\(n=3,4\\).**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n   \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n   – \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\).  \n   – \\(k=1\\): the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n   – \\(k=3\\): Lemma 4.1.  \n   The value \\(k=2\\) is impossible: with exactly two sunny lines there is only one non‑sunny line, which can cover at most one of the three points \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1)\\) that are not on the diagonal \\(y=x\\); consequently at least two of them remain uncovered.  \n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_4\\) has ten points.  \n   – \\(k=0\\): the four vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,4\\).  \n   – \\(k=1\\): take the three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\) (the last one has slope \\(-1\\)) and add the sunny line through \\((3,2)\\) and \\((4,1)\\).  \n   – \\(k=2\\): keep the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) and add the sunny lines \\(y=x-1\\) (through \\((2,1),(3,2)\\)) and \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) (through \\((2,2),(4,1)\\)).  \n   – \\(k=3\\): Lemma 4.2.  \n   The value \\(k=4\\) would mean that all four lines are sunny, which is impossible because a sunny line can meet at most one point of the column \\(C\\); with four sunny lines we could meet at most four points of \\(C\\) while \\(|C|=4\\), leaving the point \\((1,4)\\) uncovered.  \n\n7. **Collecting the results.**  \n\n   * For \\(n=3\\) the admissible numbers are \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n   * For \\(n=4\\) the admissible numbers are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge5\\) the admissible numbers are exactly the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\nHence the answer stated in the boxed formula above is complete and rigorous.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
