et been used.  \n     The interior point is chosen as follows.\n\n     *If \\(a\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\)* we take \\((a-1,a-1)\\); the line \\(\\ell_a\\) then has slope \\(1\\).\n\n     *If \\(a>\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\)* we take the point\n     \\[\n     \\bigl(2,\\;n+2-a\\bigr)\\in I .\n     \\]\n     Its coordinates satisfy \\(2+(n+2-a)\\le n+1\\) because \\(a\\ge\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor+1\\).  \n     The slope of \\(\\ell_a\\) is\n     \\[\n     m_a=\\frac{\\,n+2-a-1\\,}{\\,2-a\\,}= \\frac{n+1-a}{2-a},\n     \\]\n     which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (the denominator is non\u2011zero and the equality\n     \\(\\frac{n+1-a}{2-a}=-1\\) would give \\(n+1-a= a-2\\), i.e. \\(2a=n+3\\), impossible because the left\u2011hand side is even while the right\u2011hand side is odd).\n\n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_0,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are distinct, all are sunny, and they contain\n\n   * every point of the row \\(R\\) (by construction);\n   * every point of the column \\(C\\) (each \\(\\ell_a\\) meets \\(C\\) only at \\((1,1)\\), which already lies on \\(\\ell_0\\));\n   * every interior point (each interior point appears either on \\(\\ell_0\\) or on exactly one of the \\(\\ell_a\\)).\n\n   Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the same three lines \\(\\{y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=-2x+5\\}\\) give a concrete example.\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Let a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(m=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   *If \\(m\\ge 2\\)* we may assume (by rotating the picture) that the two non\u2011sunny lines are the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   They already cover the whole set \\(C\\cup R\\).  \n   The remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines are free to cover the interior set \\(I\\).  \n   Since \\(|I|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\) and a sunny line can contain arbitrarily many interior points, **any** \\(k\\le n\\) is compatible with \\(m\\ge 2\\).\n\n   *If \\(m=1\\)* (i.e. \\(k=n-1\\)).  \n   By symmetry we may take the unique non\u2011sunny line to be the vertical line \\(x=1\\); it covers the whole column \\(C\\).  \n   Every sunny line can meet the bottom row \\(R\\) in **at most one** point (otherwise it would be horizontal).  \n   Because there are exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, they can meet the \\(n-1\\) points \\((2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) only one\u2011to\u2011one.  \n   Consequently each interior point of \\(I\\) can belong to at most one sunny line, so the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most \\(n-1\\).  \n   For \\(n\\ge 5\\) we have \\(|I|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2>n-1\\); therefore a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) is **impossible** when \\(n\\ge5\\).  \n   For \\(n=4\\) we have \\(|I|=3=n-1\\); the following explicit configuration shows that \\(k=3\\) is attainable:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell_V : x=1 \\quad(\\text{non\u2011sunny}),\\\\\n   &\\ell_1 : y=x-1 \\quad\\text{through }(2,1),(3,2),\\\\\n   &\\ell_2 : y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 \\quad\\text{through }(2,2),(4,1),\\\\\n   &\\ell_3 : y=-2x+7 \\quad\\text{through }(2,3),(3,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n5. **All intermediate values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *For \\(n\\ge5\\).*  \n   \u2013 The case \\(k=0\\) is given by the vertical family of Section\u202f3.  \n   \u2013 For any \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) we start with the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) (they already cover \\(C\\cup R\\)).  \n     The interior set \\(I\\) is split into \\(k\\) non\u2011empty groups; each group is joined by a single sunny line (any two distinct points of a group determine a line, and because all points of \\(I\\) have both coordinates \\(\\ge2\\) the resulting slope can never be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n     Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   \u2013 For \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n=4\\) we have the construction of Step\u202f4; for \\(n\\ge5\\) the impossibility proved in Step\u202f4 shows that no such configuration exists.\n\n   \u2013 For \\(k=n\\) we use the construction of Step\u202f3.\n\n6. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of six points.  \n   \u2013 \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\).  \n   \u2013 \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines\n     \\[\n     y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\n     \\]\n     which together contain all six points.  \n   \u2013 No configuration with exactly one or two sunny lines exists: with only one non\u2011sunny line we could cover at most two points of the row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid a\\ge2\\}\\), leaving at least one uncovered; with two sunny lines each can meet the row in at most one point, so at most two row points are covered, again impossible. Hence the admissible set is \\(\\{0,3\\}\\).\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  \n   \u2013 \\(k=0\\): the four vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,4\\).  \n   \u2013 \\(k=1\\): take the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\) and add the sunny line through \\((3,2)\\) and \\((4,1)\\).  \n   \u2013 \\(k=2\\): keep the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) and add the sunny lines \\(y=x-1\\) (through \\((2,1),(3,2)\\)) and \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) (through \\((2,2),(4,1)\\)).  \n   \u2013 \\(k=3\\): the explicit configuration displayed in Step\u202f4.  \n   \u2013 \\(k=4\\) is impossible: a sunny line can meet the column \\(C\\) in at most one point, so four sunny lines can meet at most four points of \\(C\\); the point \\((1,4)\\) would remain uncovered.  \n\n   Hence the admissible set for \\(n=4\\) is \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n7. **Collecting the results.**  \n\n   Combining the constructions and the impossibility arguments we obtain exactly the families stated in the Verdict.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\]\nand \\(I=T_n\\setminus(C\\cup R)\\).  \nWe have  \n\n\\[\n|C|=|R|=n,\\qquad |C\\cup R|=2n-1,\\qquad |I|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Properties of a sunny line)\n\nA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(C\\), at most one point of \\(R\\), and at most one point of each diagonal \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=d\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  If a line contained two points of \\(C\\) it would be vertical, i.e. slope \\(\\infty\\); similarly two points of \\(R\\) would make it horizontal (slope \\(0\\)); two points with the same sum would give slope \\(-1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence of a configuration with no sunny lines)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence of a configuration with all lines sunny for \\(n\\ge5\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge5\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Construction.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_0:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(a\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define a line \\(\\ell_a\\) as follows.\n\n\\[\n\\ell_a=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{the line through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(a-1,a-1), & 2\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor,\\\\[4pt]\n\\text{the line through }(a,1)\\text{ and }(2,n+2-a), & \n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le a\\le n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nAll points used in the definition belong to \\(T_n\\):\nfor the second case we have \\(2+(n+2-a)\\le n+1\\) because \\(a\\ge\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor+1\\).\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_a\\) in the first case is \\(1\\); in the second case it equals  \n\n\\[\nm_a=\\frac{n+2-a-1}{2-a}= \\frac{n+1-a}{2-a},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (the denominator is non\u2011zero, and the equation\n\\(\\frac{n+1-a}{2-a}=-1\\) would give \\(2a=n+3\\), impossible because the left\u2011hand side is even while the right\u2011hand side is odd).\n\nThus every \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_0,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \n\n* Row points: each \\(\\ell_a\\) contains the row point \\((a,1)\\); \\(\\ell_0\\) contains \\((1,1)\\).  \n* Column points: the only column point on a sunny line is \\((1,1)\\), which lies on \\(\\ell_0\\); all other column points are covered because they belong to the row points \\((a,1)\\) after a rotation of the picture (the set \\(C\\) is symmetric to \\(R\\)).  \n* Interior points: every interior point either lies on \\(\\ell_0\\) (the points \\((i,i)\\) with \\(2i\\le n+1\\)) or is the second point used in the definition of some \\(\\ell_a\\). Hence all points of \\(I\\) are covered.\n\nTherefore the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover the whole set \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\))\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (\\(k=n-1\\)).  \nThen there is a single non\u2011sunny line; by a rotation we may take it to be the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nIt covers the whole column \\(C\\).\n\nEach sunny line can meet the bottom row \\(R\\) in at most one point (otherwise it would be horizontal).  \nSince there are exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, they can meet the \\(n-1\\) points \\((2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) only one\u2011to\u2011one.  \nConsequently a sunny line contains **at most one** interior point of \\(I\\); otherwise it would contain two points of the same diagonal and would have slope \\(-1\\).  \nHence the total number of interior points that can be covered is at most \\(n-1\\).\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\n|I|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2>n-1,\n\\]\n\nso not all interior points can be covered \u2013 a contradiction. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Existence of a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nLet \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThey cover \\(C\\cup R\\).  \nThe interior set \\(I\\) has cardinality \\(|I|\\ge k\\).  \nPartition \\(I\\) into \\(k\\) non\u2011empty subsets \\(I_1,\\dots ,I_k\\).  \nFor each \\(j\\) pick two (possibly equal) points of \\(I_j\\); the line through them is sunny because both coordinates are at least \\(2\\) and the two points cannot have the same \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate, the same \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate, nor the same sum.  \nThese \\(k\\) sunny lines together with the two non\u2011sunny lines give a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (The case \\(n=4\\))\n\nThe following four lines are distinct, three of them are sunny, and together they contain all points of \\(T_4\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell_V : x=1 \\quad(\\text{non\u2011sunny}),\\\\\n&\\ell_1 : y=x-1 \\quad\\text{through }(2,1),(3,2),\\\\\n&\\ell_2 : y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 \\quad\\text{through }(2,2),(4,1),\\\\\n&\\ell_3 : y=-2x+7 \\quad\\text{through }(2,3),(3,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k=3\\) is attainable for \\(n=4\\).  \nTogether with Lemma\u202f2, Lemma\u202f5 (which gives \\(k=0,1,2\\)) and the simple counting argument that four sunny lines cannot cover the whole column \\(C\\), we obtain that the admissible values for \\(n=4\\) are exactly \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 7 (The case \\(n=3\\))\n\nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\nare sunny, distinct, and together contain the six points of \\(T_3\\); hence \\(k=3\\) is possible.  \nLemma\u202f2 gives \\(k=0\\).  \nIf a configuration had exactly one or two sunny lines, then there would be at most one non\u2011sunny line; such a line can meet the row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid a\\ge2\\}\\) in at most one point, leaving at least one point of that row uncovered. Hence \\(k=1,2\\) are impossible. Therefore the admissible set for \\(n=3\\) is \\(\\{0,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,3\\}\\) (Lemma\u202f7).  \n* For \\(n=4\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) (Lemma\u202f6 together with Lemmas\u202f2 and\u202f5).  \n* Let \\(n\\ge5\\).  \n  \u2013 Lemma\u202f2 gives \\(k=0\\).  \n  \u2013 Lemma\u202f5 gives every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n  \u2013 Lemma\u202f4 shows that \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.  \n  \u2013 Lemma\u202f3 provides a configuration with \\(k=n\\).  \n\nThus for \\(n\\ge5\\) the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is \\(\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nk\\in\\{0,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{array}}\n\\]"
