in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad \nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\]\nand \\(I=T_n\\setminus(C\\cup R)\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|C|=|R|=n,\\qquad |C\\cup R|=2n-1,\\qquad \n|I|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Restrictions for a sunny line)\n\nA sunny line contains at most one point of each of the sets \\(C\\), \\(R\\) and each diagonal \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=d\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  If a line contained two points of \\(C\\) it would be vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)); two points of \\(R\\) would make it horizontal (slope \\(0\\)); two points with the same sum would give slope \\(-1\\).  Hence a sunny line cannot contain two such points. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All\u2011non\u2011sunny configuration)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (All\u2011sunny configuration)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Construction.*  \nChoose a **derangement** \\(\\sigma\\) of the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (i.e. a permutation with \\(\\sigma(a)\\neq a\\) for all \\(a\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\n\\qquad \n\\ell_a:\\;\\text{the line through }(1,\\sigma(a))\\text{ and }(a,1)\\;(a=2,\\dots ,n). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma(a)\\neq a\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{slope}(\\ell_a)=\\frac{1-\\sigma(a)}{a-1}= -\\frac{\\sigma(a)-1}{a-1}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nso each \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny; \\(\\ell_1\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is also sunny.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines are distinct because they have distinct column points \\((1,\\sigma(a))\\).\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n*Column points.*  \n\\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\).  \nFor \\(b\\ge2\\) there is a unique \\(a\\) with \\(\\sigma(a)=b\\); then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\n*Row points.*  \n\\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\).  \nFor \\(a\\ge2\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\) by definition.\n\n*Interior points.*  \nLet \\((x,y)\\in I\\) (so \\(x,y\\ge2\\) and \\(x+y\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n*If \\(x=y\\)*, then \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\) because \\(\\ell_1\\) is the line \\(y=x\\).\n\n*If \\(x\\neq y\\)*, set \\(a=x\\) and \\(b=y\\).  Because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, there exists a unique \\(a'\\) with \\(\\sigma(a')=b\\).  The line \\(\\ell_{a'}\\) passes through \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a',1)\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = -\\frac{b-1}{a'-1}\\,(x-a').\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) and \\(y=b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = -\\frac{b-1}{a'-1}\\,(a-a') .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b-1\\neq0\\) (because \\(b\\ge2\\)), we obtain \\(a-a' = -(a'-1)\\), i.e. \\(a+a' = n+2\\).  The equality \\(a+a'=n+2\\) holds because the derangement \\(\\sigma\\) can be chosen to satisfy \\(\\sigma(a)=b\\iff a+b=n+2\\); such a derangement exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (for example, pair \\(2\\) with \\(n\\), \\(3\\) with \\(n-1\\), \u2026).  Hence \\((x,y)\\) satisfies (3) and belongs to \\(\\ell_{a'}\\).\n\nThus every interior point belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  Consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover the whole set \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nLet \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nTake the two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThey already contain all points of \\(C\\cup R\\).  \nThe interior set \\(I\\) has size \\(|I|=\\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\) (see (1)).  \nPartition \\(I\\) into \\(k\\) non\u2011empty subsets \\(I_1,\\dots ,I_k\\).  \nFor each \\(j\\) pick two distinct points of \\(I_j\\); because both coordinates are at least \\(2\\), the line through them cannot be horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\).  Hence each of the \\(k\\) lines is sunny, the lines are distinct (different subsets give different lines), and together with the two non\u2011sunny lines we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\))\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line exists.  By a rotation of the plane we may suppose that the non\u2011sunny line is the vertical line \\(x=1\\); it covers the whole column \\(C\\) and no interior point.\n\nEach sunny line can meet the bottom row \\(R\\) in at most one point (otherwise it would be horizontal).  Because there are only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, they can meet at most \\(n-1\\) points of the set \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid a\\ge2\\}\\).  The point \\((1,1)\\) is already on the vertical line, so all \\(n\\) row points are covered \u2013 this part causes no contradiction.\n\nConsider the interior points.  By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line contains at most one point from each diagonal \\(\\{x+y=d\\}\\).  The vertical line contains none of them, therefore the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must together contain all \\(|I|=\\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\) interior points, using at most one point from each of the \\(n-1\\) diagonals per line.  Hence the total number of interior\u2011diagonal incidences that can be realised is at most \\((n-1)(n-1)\\).\n\nIf a diagonal contained two interior points that lie on the same sunny line, that line would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting sunniness.  Consequently **each diagonal can contribute at most one interior point to each sunny line**; the total number of interior points that can be covered is therefore at most \\((n-