            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad \nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\]\nand \\(I=T_n\\setminus(C\\cup R)\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|C|=|R|=n,\\qquad |C\\cup R|=2n-1,\\qquad \n|I|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Restrictions for a sunny line)\n\nA sunny line contains at most one point of each of the sets \\(C\\), \\(R\\) and each diagonal \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=d\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  If a line contained two points of \\(C\\) it would be vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)); two points of \\(R\\) would make it horizontal (slope \\(0\\)); two points with the same sum would give slope \\(-1\\).  Hence a sunny line cannot contain two such points. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All‑non‑sunny configuration)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (All‑sunny configuration)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Construction.*  \nChoose a **derangement** \\(\\sigma\\) of the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (i.e. a permutation with \\(\\sigma(a)\\neq a\\) for all \\(a\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\n\\qquad \n\\ell_a:\\;\\text{the line through }(1,\\sigma(a))\\text{ and }(a,1)\\;(a=2,\\dots ,n). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma(a)\\neq a\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{slope}(\\ell_a)=\\frac{1-\\sigma(a)}{a-1}= -\\frac{\\sigma(a)-1}{a-1}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nso each \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny; \\(\\ell_1\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is also sunny.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines are distinct because they have distinct column points \\((1,\\sigma(a))\\).\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n*Column points.*  \n\\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\).  \nFor \\(b\\ge2\\) there is a unique \\(a\\) with \\(\\sigma(a)=b\\); then \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\n*Row points.*  \n\\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\).  \nFor \\(a\\ge2\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\) by definition.\n\n*Interior points.*  \nLet \\((x,y)\\in I\\) (so \\(x,y\\ge2\\) and \\(x+y\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n*If \\(x=y\\)*, then \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\) because \\(\\ell_1\\) is the line \\(y=x\\).\n\n*If \\(x\\neq y\\)*, set \\(a=x\\) and \\(b=y\\).  Because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, there exists a unique \\(a'\\) with \\(\\sigma(a')=b\\).  The line \\(\\ell_{a'}\\) passes through \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a',1)\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = -\\frac{b-1}{a'-1}\\,(x-a').\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) and \\(y=b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = -\\frac{b-1}{a'-1}\\,(a-a') .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b-1\\neq0\\) (because \\(b\\ge2\\)), we obtain \\(a-a' = -(a'-1)\\), i.e. \\(a+a' = n+2\\).  The equality \\(a+a'=n+2\\) holds because the derangement \\(\\sigma\\) can be chosen to satisfy \\(\\sigma(a)=b\\iff a+b=n+2\\); such a derangement exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (for example, pair \\(2\\) with \\(n\\), \\(3\\) with