line contains none of them, therefore the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines must together contain all \\(|I|=\\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\) interior points, using at most one point from each of the \\(n-1\\) diagonals per line.  Hence the total number of interior‑diagonal incidences that can be realised is at most \\((n-1)(n-1)\\).\n\nIf a diagonal contained two interior points that lie on the same sunny line, that line would have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting sunniness.  Consequently **each diagonal can contribute at most one interior point to each sunny line**; the total number of interior points that can be covered is therefore at most \\((n-1)\\) (the number of diagonals).  Since  \n\n\\[\n|I|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 > n-1 \\qquad (n\\ge5),\n\\]\n\nthe interior points cannot all be covered.  Hence a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines does not exist for any \\(n\\ge5\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (Small values of \\(n\\))\n\n*\\(n=3\\).*  \nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered either by the three vertical lines (giving \\(k=0\\)) or by the three sunny lines obtained from Lemma 3 with the derangement \\(\\sigma(2)=3,\\sigma(3)=2\\):\n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n\\]\n\nNo configuration with \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=2\\) exists: with only one non‑sunny line at most one row point can be covered, leaving another row point uncovered.\n\n*\\(n=4\\).*  \nUsing Lemma 3 with the derangement \\(\\sigma(2)=4,\\sigma(3)=2,\\sigma(4)=3\\) we obtain three sunny lines together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\); this yields \\(k=3\\).  \nLemma 4 supplies configurations for \\(k=0,1,2\\).  \nA configuration with \\(k=4\\) is impossible because a sunny line containing the column point \\((1,1)\\) cannot contain any other row point, so at most three row points could be covered by the remaining three lines, leaving one uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is possible (Lemma 2).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is possible (Lemma 3).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration exists (Lemma 4).  \n* For \\(n\\ge5\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible (Lemma 5).  \n* The exhaustive analysis for \\(n=3,4\\) given in Lemma 6 shows that the only admissible values are those listed in the statement of the theorem.\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nk\\in\\{0,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
