stinct sunny line **or** share a line with another interior point that lies on a different diagonal.  A careful double‑counting of incidences between sunny lines and diagonals shows that with only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines at least one diagonal would have to contain two interior points on the same line, which would force the line’s slope to be \\(-1\\), contradicting sunniness.  Hence a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n7. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  The six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered either by the three vertical lines (giving \\(k=0\\)) or by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\n   \\]\n\n   which correspond to the derangement \\(\\sigma(2)=3,\\sigma(3)=2\\).  No configuration with \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=2\\) exists because a single non‑sunny line can cover at most one row point, leaving an uncovered row point.\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  Using the same derangement idea we obtain three sunny lines (the lines through \\((1,2)\\)–\\((3,1)\\), \\((1,3)\\)–\\((4,1)\\) and \\((1,4)\\)–\\((2,1)\\)) together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\); this gives \\(k=3\\).  Configurations with \\(k=0,1,2\\) are obtained from Lemma 5, and \\(k=4\\) is impossible because a sunny line containing \\((1,1)\\) cannot contain any other row point, so at most three row points could be covered by the remaining three lines, leaving one uncovered.\n\n8. **Collecting the results.**  \n\n   Combining Lemma 2 ( \\(k=0\\) ), Lemma 3 ( \\(k=n\\) ), Lemma 5 (all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) ), and Lemma 6 (the impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\) together with the explicit small‑\\(n\\) analysis) yields exactly the families stated in the Verdict.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad \nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\]\nand \\(I=T_n\\setminus(C\\cup R)\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|C|=|R|=n,\\qquad |C\\cup R|=2n-1,\\qquad \n|I|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Restrictions for a sunny line)\n\nA sunny line contains at most one point of each of the sets \\(C\\), \\(R\\) and each diagonal \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=d\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  If a line contained two points of \\(C\\) it would be vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)); two points of \\(R\\) would make it horizontal (slope \\(0\\)); two points with the same sum would give slope \\(-1\\).  Hence a sunny line cannot contain two such points. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All‑non‑sunny configuration)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (All‑sunny configuration)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Construction.*  \nChoose a **derangement** \\(\\sigma\\) of the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (i.e. a permutation with \\(\\sigma(a)\\neq a\\) for all \\(a\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\n\\qquad \n\\ell_a:\\;\\text{the line through }(1,\\sigma(a))\\text{ and }(a,1)\\;(a=2,\\dots ,n). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma(a)\\neq a\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{slope}(\\ell_a)=\\frac{1-\\sigma(a)}{a-1}= -\\frac{\\sigma(a)-1}{a-1}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nso each \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny; \\(\\ell_1\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is also sunny.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines are distinct because they have distinct column points \\((1,\\sigma(a))\\).\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n*Column points.*  \n\\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\).  \nFor \\(b\\ge2\\) there is a unique \\(a\\) wit