            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad \nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n\\qquad \nI=T_n\\setminus(C\\cup R).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|C|=|R|=n\\) and  \n\n\\[\n|I|=\\sum_{s=4}^{n+1}(s-3)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Restrictions for a sunny line)\n\nA sunny line contains at most one point of each column, each row and each diagonal\n\\(\\{x+y=d\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two points of the same column it would be vertical (\\(m=\\infty\\));\ntwo points of the same row would make it horizontal (\\(m=0\\));\ntwo points with the same sum would give slope \\(-1\\).  Hence a sunny line cannot contain two such points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All‑non‑sunny configuration)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nAssume \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThey cover all points of \\(C\\cup R\\).  \nThe interior set \\(I\\) has size given by (1).  Partition \\(I\\) into \\(k\\) non‑empty blocks\n\\(I_1,\\dots ,I_k\\).  \n\nFor a fixed block \\(I_j\\) choose two distinct points\n\\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in I_j\\) with \\(x_1\\neq x_2\\) and \\(y_1\\neq y_2\\) (possible because every block contains at least two points when \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \nThe line through them has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nbecause both numerator and denominator are non‑zero and\n\\(y_2-y_1\\neq -(x_2-x_1)\\) (otherwise the two points would lie on a diagonal of slope \\(-1\\), which is impossible inside \\(I\\)).  \nHence the line is sunny.  \n\nDifferent blocks give different lines, so we obtain exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\nTogether with the two non‑sunny lines (2) we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nLet \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nConsider the non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n\\[\nd:\\;x+y=n+1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIt contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\); in particular it covers the column point \\((1,n)\\) and the row point \\((n,1)\\).\n\nAdd the two further non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  \nThese three lines already cover all points of \\(C\\cup R\\) except \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\), which lie on \\(d\\).\n\nThe interior points not belonging to \\(d\\) are precisely those of \\(I\\) with sum \\(\\le n\\); their number is\n\n\\[\n|I|- (n-2)=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2-(n-2)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-3)}2 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) the quantity in (4) is at most \\(n-4\\); therefore we can split this set into \\(n-4\\) non‑empty blocks and apply the procedure of Lemma 3 to each block.  \nThus we obtain \\(n-4\\) sunny lines, together with the three non‑sunny lines (3) and (2), i.e. a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=n-(\\text{number of non‑sunny lines})=n-3=n-1\n\\]\n\nsunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n*Step 1 – one column and one row per line.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most one column point; because there are exactly \\(n\\) column points, every line must contain **exactly one** of them.  The same argument with rows shows that each line also contains exactly one row point.\n\nConsequently there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the line \\(\\ell_b\\) contains the column point \\((1,b)\\) and the row point \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\).  \nSince a line through \\((1,b)\\) and \\((\\sigma(b),1)\\) would be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\) precisely when \\(\\sigma(b)=b\\), the fact that all lines are sunny forces  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(b)\\neq b\\qquad\\text{for every }b,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(\\sigma\\) is a **derangement**.\n\n*Step 2 – the product invariant.*  \nWriting the equation of \\(\\ell_b\\) in intercept form gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{x-1}{\\sigma(b)-1}+\\frac{y-1}{b-1}=1 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying out we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(b-1)(x-1)+(\\sigma(b)-1)(y-1)=(\\sigma(b)-1)(b-1). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor a point \\((x,y)\\in I\\) (so \\(x,y\\ge2\\)) the left‑hand side of (5) is an integer; rearranging yields the **product condition**\n\n\\[\n(x-1)(y-1)=(\\sigma(b)-1)(b-1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus an interior point lies on \\(\\ell_b\\) **iff** its product \\((x-1)(y-1)\\) equals the product \\(p_b:=(\\sigma(b)-1)(b-1)\\) attached to the line.\n\n*Step 3 – counting distinct products.*  \nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a derangement, the numbers \\(p_b\\;(b=1,\\dots ,n)\\) are pairwise distinct: if \\(p_{b_1}=p_{b_2}\\) then\n\\((\\sigma(b_1)-1)(b_1-1)=(\\sigma(b_2)-1)(b_2-1)\\); since the factors \\(b_i-1\\) are distinct, we would obtain \\(\\sigma(b_1)=\\sigma(b_2)\\), contradicting that \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.\n\nHence the \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover interior points whose products belong to at most \\(n\\) distinct values.\n\n*Step 4 – too many interior products.*  \nThe set of interior points \\(I\\) contains \\(|I|=\\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\) elements (see (1)).  \nFor \\(n\\ge4\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2>n,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2-n=\\frac{(n-4)(n-3)}2\\ge1\\).  \nConsequently there are **more** interior points than distinct products \\(p_b\\); by the pigeon‑hole principle at least one interior point has a product that does not appear among the \\(p_b\\) and therefore cannot lie on any of the \\(n\\) sunny lines.  This contradicts the assumption that the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge4\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 6 (The case \\(n=3\\))\n\nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are\n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following families of three distinct lines realise the four possible values of \\(k\\):\n\n* \\(k=0\\): \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) (all non‑sunny).\n* \\(k=1\\): \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\) (the only sunny line is \\(y=x\\)).\n* \\(k=2\\): \\(x=1,\\;(1,2)-(3,1),\\;(2,1)-(1,3)\\) (the two slant lines are sunny).\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n  \\]\n\nThus every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) occurs for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is possible (Lemma 2).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration exists (Lemma 3).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) exists (Lemma 4).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) does **not** exist (Lemma 5).  \n* For \\(n=3\\) the four values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are all attainable (Lemma 6).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2pt]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
